<para>Some quadratic expressions have no linear terms. For questions 6 – 7, find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph representing each equation:</para>
<para>\(y=x^2-25\)</para>
<para>\(y=x^2+16\)</para>
<para>(Note it is possible for the graph to not intersect the \(x\)-axis.) If you get stuck, try graphing the equations.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Find the \(x\)-intercepts and the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\).</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> What are the \(x\)-intercepts for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)? </item>
  </list>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((5,0)\),\((-5,0)\)</para>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex for the equation \(y=x^2-25\)? </item>
  </list>
  
